<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06WV">Remark <span data-tag="06WV">9.6.7.5</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="06WT" href="/tag/06WT">9.6.7.4</a>, suppose that $\sigma = s_{1}^{1}(f)$ is left-degenerate and that $\sigma ' = s^{1}_{0}( g' )$ is right-degenerate (or, more generally, that $f'$ and $g$ are isomorphisms). In this case, the reduction steps in the proof are unnecessary, and the argument is reversible: $f$ is left orthogonal to $g'$ if and only if the restriction map $\operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^2, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( \sigma , \sigma ' ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) }( h, h' )$ is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
